From 29b26196ce61f5625441c004c586e0af3ba563ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mirko Vogt <foss@mirko.in>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 23:50:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] build: resolve target constants for buildvm with the target
 compiler

buildvm is compiled with the host compiler, but the DynASM-generated
backend passes offsets and sizes of the LuaJIT core data structures to
dasm_put(). These are only correct if the host compiler uses the same
pointer size and alignment rules as the target. Cross-compiling for a
32 bit target on a 64 bit host therefore required a 32 bit host
toolchain (HOST_CC="gcc -m32") and a kernel which executes 32 bit
binaries. A host with CONFIG_IA32_EMULATION disabled cannot build
LuaJIT for 32 bit targets at all.

Resolve these constants with the target compiler instead:

- host/genoffsets.lua rewrites every dasm_put() call in the DynASM
  output into asm statements which make the compiler emit the value of
  each constant argument, together with a flag whether the argument is
  constant (__builtin_constant_p).
- buildvm.c is compiled with the target compiler (-S only, the output
  is never assembled or run) with LJ_BUILDVM_PROBE defined, which
  includes this variant of the backend. Optimization is disabled so
  that every probe appears exactly once.
- The values are substituted into the DynASM output, which becomes
  host/buildvm_arch.h. Arguments which are not constant (they depend
  on the bytecode opcode or a loop variable) and arguments in code the
  compiler dropped are left unchanged. A non-constant argument which
  depends on the data layout is rejected.
- offsetof() and the DynASM type macros are redefined in the resulting
  header to reference an undefined function, so a remaining reachable
  use fails when linking buildvm instead of being evaluated by the
  host compiler.

lj_arch.h derives the pointer size of a PowerPC target from _LP64,
which is a property of the compiler in use, not of the target. The
host compile of buildvm therefore has to be told the pointer size of
the target: LJ_ARCH_BITS can now be predefined for PowerPC, like
LJ_ARCH_ENDIAN already can, and the Makefile passes the value found
in the target configuration. Otherwise a 64 bit host builds buildvm
for a ppc64 target, which has no JIT compiler, and the generated
files do not match the 32 bit target build.

The pointer size check in buildvm is removed, and the host tools are
built with the default host compiler. The MSVC and console build
scripts are unaffected: they generate host/buildvm_arch.h with DynASM
directly and compile buildvm as before.

Verified by comparing lj_vm.S, lj_bcdef.h, lj_ffdef.h, lj_libdef.h,
lj_recdef.h, lj_folddef.h and jit/vmdef.lua against the previous
method (HOST_CC matching the pointer size of the target): the files
are byte-identical for x64 (native), x86 (gcc -m32), arm
(arm-openwrt-linux-muslgnueabi, hard float), arm64
(aarch64-openwrt-linux-musl), ppc (powerpc-openwrt-linux-musl, 8548
soft float and 464fp hard float), each built with a 64 bit host
buildvm, and for x64 and arm64 built with a 32 bit host buildvm. The
DynASM output for mips, mipsel, mips64el and riscv64 was checked for
arguments the script cannot resolve; there are none.

